bill warcola stands out in front of his  home on main street sorting through the  small mountain of what's left of his  possessions  it's everybody's partial blinds except  that flooded stuff that was under water  this is all in basement this is  generally everybody's basement his  basement filled up with water and is now  a muddy mess with what little he could  salvage and it totally filled up with  water and flooded it comes above the  floor above us when i first moved here  cola has lived in the town of manville  new jersey his entire life 68 years and  seen five different floods this was the  worst you see the garage is the peep out  sign yep you see the black line yep so  this is this flood the water level was  here  as bad as it looks from the ground the  true scale of the destruction can only  be seen from the air a few days ago  first the town flooded then gas line  breaks set some homes on fire  in manville population 10 000 it's  estimated 500 people are now displaced  after the remnants of hurricane ida came  through and flooded the town over the  weekend  in new jersey 27 people were killed  making it one of the worst natural  disasters in the state's history it came  with a vengeance it came so fast and so  high i've never seen it as high as  it came  ever  i've lived here all my life and i've  never seen the waters as high as they  were there was no  no warning they didn't sound sirens  until after the town started flooding  many residents told me the weather is  changing and for the worse they keep  telling us their hundred year storms  their 500 year storms whatever they tell  us but  is that really the case  you know  you look at look around i mean if this  is a hundred year storm and yes it works  on averages but  i think we're exceeding the average  quite a bit in this town you know the  storms are getting  more and more intense in the area  in a town that bore the brunt of a  deadly storm with a long recovery and  rebuilding process ahead  gabriel lozando al jazeera manville new  jersey
 

